 /wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Rankine-4-%C2%A9-Dirk-Skiba-scaled-for-website
 .jpg
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org/wp-content/uploads/202
 6/07/Rankine-4-%C2%A9-Dirk-Skiba-scaled-for-website.jpg
UID:fzRq0A0DZ8KFT5pXTwRe8
SUMMARY:Inprint Claudia Rankine Reading
DTSTAMP:20260816T033420Z
DTSTART:20260914T193000Z
DESCRIPTION: \nGeneral admission tickets will go on sale on Tuesday\, Augus
	t 25\, 2026 at 12 pm.\nSeason tickets are on sale here. \nClaudia Rankine 
	will read from her new hybrid-genre work Triage\, followed by an on-stage 
	conversation led by poet and Rice University professor Phillip B. Williams
	. The evening will conclude with a book sale and signing. The event is pre
	sented as part of the 2026/2027 Inprint Margarett Root Brown Reading Serie
	s.  \nWhether writing about intimacy or alienation\, Claudia Rankine posse
	sses a voice of unrelenting candor that “shimmers with wisdom and fury” (T
	he Telegraph). A MacArthur Fellow\, Rankine is the author of five collecti
	ons of poetry\, including her bestselling Citizen: An American Lyric\, whi
	ch explores what it means to be an American citizen in a “post-racial” soc
	iety. A defining text for our time\, Citizen won the National Book Critics
	 Circle Award\, the NAACP Image Award\, the PEN Open Book Award\, and the 
	Los Angeles Times Book Prize. She is also the author of three plays\, incl
	uding Provenance of Beauty: A South Bronx Travelogue\, which is performed 
	on a bus ride through the Bronx\; and is the editor of several anthologies
	\, including The Racial Imaginary: Writers on Race in the Life of the Mind
	. \nRankine returns to Inprint with her genre-defying new work Triage\, wh
	ich collides the lyric with narrative\, memoir\, image\, and essay to offe
	r her most personal and emotionally resonant writing yet. Triage follows t
	he turbulent friendship between two characters\, the narrator and the theo
	rist\, self-identified sisters struggling to define their wounded historie
	s and their shared but separate lives. According to Jamaica Kincaid\, “Cla
	udia Rankine’s remarkable\, outstanding odyssey—an account of our present\
	, which is also our past\, which is always our future—is beautifully rende
	red in a kaleidoscope of images and sensations. . . . Triage is exquisite.
	” \n\n\nhttps://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-claudia-rankine-reading-2
	/\n\nFrom Inprint Houston
URL:https://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-claudia-rankine-reading-2/
LOCATION:Brockman Hall for Opera\, Rice University\, Entrance #18 off Rice 
	Boulevard\, Houston\, TX\, 77005
